WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.360
This is one of the most famous&nbsp;
experiments in linguistics.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.280
One of these shapes is called “bouba”.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:08.320
The other is called&nbsp;
“kiki”. Which is which?

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.680
This was invented in 1929,&nbsp;
using slightly different words,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:13.600
and it’s been refined over time.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:16.320
For most people, the&nbsp;
pointy shape is "kiki"

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.480
and the rounded shape is "bouba."

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.160
The majority of studies…

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:23.040
Wow, that is a lot of citations.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:27.440
The majority of studies find that&nbsp;
pointy shapes are more associated with

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.840
unvoiced plosives and front&nbsp;
vowels: so “ta, pi, ka”,

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.000
and round shapes are associated with

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.400
voiced plosives, nasals, and&nbsp;
back vowels, so, “bou, gǝ, no”.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.080
Okay, sure, but that’s&nbsp;
just English, right?

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.080
We have words like&nbsp;
‘point’ and ‘balloon’,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.400
maybe we’re just copying the&nbsp;
associations from the words&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:45.360
we already know.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.120
One of the founders&nbsp;
of modern linguistics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.960
has an entire theory named after him,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:50.640
about there being no&nbsp;
relation between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.400
form of a word and what it represents.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.600
But: there’s a study&nbsp;
where English speakers

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.240
were given pairs of words&nbsp;
in a language from Peru.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:00:59.040
In each pair,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.080
one word was for a bird,&nbsp;
and one was for a fish.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.400
And the English speakers,&nbsp;
who didn’t know the language,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.880
who knew nothing even&nbsp;
close to the language…

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.240
they could sort those&nbsp;
words into birds and fish&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.160
a little bit better than chance.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:16.000
Not well, but out of hundreds of people&nbsp;
answering thousands of questions,

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.560
they got 58% right. That is a&nbsp;
statistically significant result.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.760
And there’s another study where&nbsp;
Hebrew speakers were given pairs of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.480
Chinese characters with&nbsp;
opposite definitions,

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:30.000
and asked to match up the&nbsp;
characters and definitions.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.440
Again, slightly better than&nbsp;
chance, about 55% right.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.400
And if people who speak&nbsp;
different languages have even

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.840
a slight ability to figure out&nbsp;
completely unfamiliar words,

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:42.080
well, that raises the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.040
do humans have some sort&nbsp;
of built-in associations

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.160
between sounds and symbols&nbsp;
and things in the real world?

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:52.800
“Bouba” and “kiki” have been tested&nbsp;
in a lot of languages, and, yeah,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.400
there seems to be something there,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.080
what researchers call a type of&nbsp;
"crossmodal correspondence" or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.360
"sound symbolism."

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:02.160
Correlations between&nbsp;
phonemes, the sounds we make,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.320
and traits like shape, texture,&nbsp;
brightness, size, or even taste.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:10.400
Maybe that’s down to cross-activation&nbsp;
between brain regions:

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.240
researchers into synaesthesia&nbsp;
have spent a long time on that.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:16.400
Or it could be repeated association.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.480
An elephant makes a&nbsp;
deeper sound than a mouse.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.240
A large dog usually barks lower&nbsp;
and longer than a small dog.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:23.920
If you shout into a big, round cave,

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:27.360
it’ll reflect back deep,&nbsp;
round, resonating tones.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:29.840
Shout into a tiny cave with&nbsp;
a lot of sharp angles in it

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.600
and you’ll hear higher, sharper tones.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.480
Something hard and brittle is more&nbsp;
likely to make a sharp ‘kh’ sound,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.520
something like ‘kiki’, when you&nbsp;
hit it or break it or shatter it;

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:43.760
something soft and round is more&nbsp;
likely to make a noise like “bouba”.

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.880
I need to stress this is one&nbsp;
theory from a couple of papers,

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.480
don’t take this as gospel, it’s&nbsp;
right to be skeptical about that.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.240
Plus, it doesn’t always work.

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.400
A paper from 1975 shows the results of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.360
bouba-kiki style test on Songe&nbsp;
speakers in Papua New Guinea,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.960
where the results were like they were&nbsp;
picking at random. No preference.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.000
And in 2017, another test on Syuba&nbsp;
speakers in Nepal, again: no preference.

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.880
The likely reason is that&nbsp;
the nonsense words they chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:10.720
could not exist in those languages.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.360
It’d be like giving English&nbsp;
speakers a test to choose between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:14.880
“ŋoba” and “tlet”.

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.160
You can’t start a word&nbsp;
with ŋ or tl in English,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:19.680
so the choice doesn’t make sense.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.720
The frustrating thing is: there&nbsp;
isn’t much data on the failures.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.880
Researchers often don’t&nbsp;
publish their negative results,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:30.800
and besides it’s very difficult and&nbsp;
very expensive to give linguistic tests

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.600
to people who’ve never been exposed&nbsp;
to any of the major world languages.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:38.240
But according to all those studies,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.760
that bouba/kiki distinction&nbsp;
is true for most people.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.120
There may be a link between some&nbsp;
sounds and some real-world properties.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.840
And that may be why English&nbsp;
speakers were able to distinguish

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.320
birds and fish in that&nbsp;
Peruvian language:

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.080
the birds’ names had more high,&nbsp;
front, non‐rounded vowels:

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:56.720
/i/ and /e/, they had&nbsp;
more “sharp sounds”.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.840
And while there are&nbsp;
plenty of exceptions,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:04.000
birds have more sharp beaks and&nbsp;
claws than your average fish does.

